<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe</h1><p class="st-meta">1470 CE – 1975 CE · capital São Tomé</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portuguese island colony from 1470 to 1975, once the world's largest sugar producer, then a leading cocoa producer, independent after the Carnation Revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Portuguese explorers João de Santarém and Pêro Escobar found the uninhabited islands around 1470. A first attempt at settlement from 1485 under João de Paiva failed, and the first successful settlement came in 1493, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II of Portugal named Álvaro Caminha captain-major of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a>. Príncipe was settled from 1500. The settlers included criminals, orphans and Jewish children taken from their parents to be raised as Christians.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Slaves imported from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> worked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, and by the middle of the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> was the world's largest producer of sugar. Competition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and frequent slave revolts weakened sugar in the first decade of the 17th century, and the economy turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which stayed mostly with the local mestiço population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> occupied São Tomé from 1641 to 1648. In 1753 the capital moved to Santo António on Príncipe and the islands were ruled as one colony; the capital returned to São Tomé in 1852. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and then cocoa, grown on large estates called roças, became the main crops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Exiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a> founded the Marxist Movement for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome-and-principe">São Tomé and Príncipe</a> (MLSTP) in 1972, intending to create an independent nation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in Portugal and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of its African colonies. On 12 July 1975 the new Portuguese regime granted independence to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sao-tome-and-principe">São Tomé and Príncipe</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-sao-tome-and-principe.jpg" alt="Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe" width="330" height="288"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_São_Tomé_and_Príncipe_(1951–1975).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1470 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>São Tomé</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>São Tomé and Príncipe</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>São Tomé and Príncipe (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1722&amp;focus=portuguese-sao-tome-and-principe" aria-label="Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 2165 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5187 2421L5187 2421"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5187 2421h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5187 2421h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5187 2421h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks São Tomé. <a href="/atlas?year=1722&amp;focus=po